          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/commercial-treadmills",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What actually makes a treadmill \"commercial-grade\"?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A commercial-grade treadmill is built and warrantied for continuous, all-day use rather than a few hours per week. That typically means an AC drive motor instead of a DC motor, a duty-cycle rating for 24/7 gym operation, a heavier fixed or reinforced frame, and a commercial-tier warranty from the manufacturer, not just marketing language calling a machine \"commercial-quality.\"",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is an AC motor really better than a DC motor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For continuous heavy use, yes. AC motors are built to run for extended periods without overheating or losing torque, which is why virtually every true commercial gym treadmill uses one. DC motors, common on home-tier machines, are perfectly reliable for typical residential use patterns but aren't designed for the sustained, all-day duty cycle of a busy gym floor.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it worth paying commercial prices for a home treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on your household's actual usage. If multiple people train daily, if you're doing long endurance sessions, or if you simply want a machine built to outlast several home-tier replacements, commercial-grade pricing can pay for itself over a decade. For lighter, occasional use a few times a week, a well-built home-tier treadmill will likely serve you just as well for meaningfully less money.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do commercial-grade treadmills come with better warranties?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Generally yes, especially on motor and frame coverage. It's common to see lifetime or 10-plus-year frame warranties and multi-year motor coverage on true commercial machines, reflecting the manufacturer's confidence in the build quality, compared to shorter frame and motor coverage windows typical of home-tier treadmills.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do commercial treadmills require special electrical or floor setup?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some do. Higher-powered AC motors on the largest commercial machines can require a dedicated circuit, and the heavier fixed frames benefit from a level, reinforced floor, particularly on upper stories. It's worth checking a specific model's electrical and flooring requirements before purchase rather than assuming standard household wiring and flooring will suffice.",
          },
        },
