      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- Lede -->
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          Every treadmill manufacturer wants you to believe their machine is
          "commercial-quality." Almost none of them actually are. True
          commercial-grade treadmills are built to a different engineering
          standard entirely, designed to run for hours a day, every day, for
          years, without the motor derating or the frame loosening under
          load. That standard costs real money, and it isn't always worth it
          for a home installation. We tested six genuinely commercial-duty
          treadmills to find out which ones justify the premium and who
          should actually be buying at this tier in the first place.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <h2 id="what-is-commercial-grade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          What "Commercial-Grade" Actually Means
        </h2>
        <p>
          The term "commercial-grade" gets used loosely across the fitness
          equipment industry, often applied to any treadmill with a slightly
          bigger motor than average. The real definition is narrower and more
          technical, and understanding it is the difference between paying a
          fair premium for genuine durability and paying a marketing tax for
          a sticker on the console.
        </p>
        <h3>AC Motors vs. DC Motors</h3>
        <p>
          This is the single biggest structural difference between a true
          commercial treadmill and a home-tier machine. AC motors are
          engineered to run continuously for extended periods without
          overheating or losing torque, which is exactly what a busy gym
          floor demands when a treadmill might be in near-constant use for
          twelve or more hours a day. DC motors, used on the overwhelming
          majority of home treadmills, are perfectly reliable for typical
          residential patterns of an hour or two a day, but they aren't
          engineered for that same sustained, all-day duty cycle.
        </p>
        <h3>Duty Cycle Ratings</h3>
        <p>
          Manufacturers assign duty cycle ratings that describe how much
          continuous use a machine is actually built to handle, and true
          commercial machines carry ratings meant for 24/7 gym operation.
          This number rarely appears prominently in marketing copy, but it's
          worth asking about directly, since it's a far more reliable
          indicator of genuine commercial-grade construction than horsepower
          alone.
        </p>
        <h3>Fixed Frames vs. Folding Frames</h3>
        <p>
          Most true commercial treadmills use a fixed, non-folding frame,
          because rigidity and long-term structural integrity matter more in
          a high-traffic setting than the ability to save floor space. A few
          machines, like the NordicTrack Commercial 2950 in this roundup,
          borrow commercial-tier components into a folding frame, which is a
          reasonable compromise for a home setting but does trade away a
          small amount of the absolute rigidity a fixed frame provides.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deck construction, belt thickness, and roller quality round out the
          rest of the commercial-grade picture. Commercial decks tend to use
          thicker, more heat-resistant belts and larger-diameter rollers that
          spread wear more evenly over time, which is part of why these
          machines carry such long frame and motor warranties in the first
          place. None of this is marketing fluff; it's genuine engineering
          built around a very different usage pattern than the average home
          buyer puts a treadmill through.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth being skeptical of the phrase "commercial-quality" when
          it appears in marketing copy without any of the specifics above
          attached to it. We've seen the term applied to treadmills with
          conventional DC motors and home-tier duty cycle ratings, simply
          because the frame is a bit heavier than average or the deck is a
          bit larger. That's not dishonest exactly, but it's a meaningfully
          different claim than the treadmills in this roundup are making. A
          genuine commercial-grade machine will typically list its duty
          cycle rating, motor type, and commercial warranty terms explicitly
          on the spec sheet, not just describe itself with the adjective. If
          a listing calls a treadmill "commercial-grade" but the details page
          doesn't specify an AC motor or a duty-cycle number, treat that as a
          sign to dig deeper before assuming it belongs in this category.
        </p>
        <p>
          Console and electronics durability are part of the picture too,
          even though they get less attention than motors and frames.
          Commercial touchscreens and button panels are built to withstand
          thousands of daily interactions from different users, with sealed
          housings that resist sweat and dust infiltration over years of
          heavy use. We noticed a tangible difference in button and
          touchscreen responsiveness after weeks of aggressive daily testing
          between the commercial-tier consoles in this roundup and typical
          home-tier displays, which tend to show wear, lag, or reduced touch
          sensitivity noticeably sooner under the same usage load.
        </p>

