        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <h2 id="delivery-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Delivery, Space, and Power Requirements
        </h2>
        <p>
          Commercial-grade treadmills demand more planning before delivery
          day than a typical home treadmill, and skipping this step is one
          of the more common regrets we hear about from first-time
          commercial-tier buyers.
        </p>
        <h3>Weight and Delivery Access</h3>
        <p>
          Fixed-frame commercial machines routinely weigh 300-400 pounds or
          more, substantially heavier than most folding home treadmills.
          Measure doorways, hallways, and any stairs between your entry point
          and the final installation room before ordering, and confirm
          whether the retailer's delivery service includes placement inside
          the home or curbside drop-off only, since a machine this heavy
          often requires professional movers either way.
        </p>
        <h3>Electrical Requirements</h3>
        <p>
          Some higher-powered AC-motor commercial treadmills draw enough
          current to require a dedicated 20-amp circuit rather than sharing a
          standard household outlet with other devices. Check the specific
          model's electrical specifications before delivery, and if a
          dedicated circuit is recommended, budget time and cost for an
          electrician if your installation room doesn't already have one.
        </p>
        <h3>Floor Reinforcement</h3>
        <p>
          A fixed-frame commercial treadmill concentrates significant weight
          in a permanent spot, and on upper floors or over unfinished
          basements, it's worth confirming the floor structure can handle
          that sustained load, particularly with the added dynamic force of
          someone running on it. This is rarely an issue on a ground-floor
          slab, but it's a real consideration for upper-level installations.
        </p>
        <h3>Permanent Footprint Planning</h3>
        <p>
          Because most true commercial machines don't fold, treat the
          installation spot as permanent from day one. Measure not just the
          treadmill's footprint but the clearance space around it,
          typically at least two feet on each side and behind the rear
          roller, both for safe dismounting and for any future service
          access.
        </p>
        <h3>Noise and Vibration in Multi-Level Homes</h3>
        <p>
          A commercial-duty AC motor and a large fixed frame transmit more
          vibration into the floor structure than a lighter home-tier
          machine, particularly during faster running. If the installation
          room sits above a bedroom, home office, or shared living space, a
          quality isolation mat rated for heavier equipment is worth
          budgeting for alongside the treadmill itself. We measured a
          noticeable drop in perceived vibration and impact noise on every
          fixed-frame machine in this roundup once a proper isolation mat was
          added underneath the full footprint, and several testers living in
          multi-story homes called it close to essential rather than
          optional at this weight class.
        </p>
        <h3>Service and Maintenance Access</h3>
        <p>
          Commercial machines are built to be serviced in place rather than
          replaced, and that means planning for a technician to access the
          motor housing, belt, and deck underside periodically over the
          life of the machine. Leave real clearance behind and beside the
          unit rather than pushing it flush against a wall or into a tight
          corner, and keep any original assembly documentation somewhere
          you can find it, since commercial-tier service visits often need
          model and serial information that's easy to lose track of years
          after installation.
        </p>

