          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is an AC motor really better than a DC motor?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For continuous heavy use, yes. AC motors are built to run for
              extended periods without overheating or losing torque, which is
              why virtually every true commercial gym treadmill uses one. DC
              motors, common on home-tier machines, are perfectly reliable
              for typical residential use patterns but aren't designed for the
              sustained, all-day duty cycle of a busy gym floor.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is it worth paying commercial prices for a home treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It depends on your household's actual usage. If multiple people
              train daily, if you're doing long endurance sessions, or if you
              simply want a machine built to outlast several home-tier
              replacements, commercial-grade pricing can pay for itself over
              a decade. For lighter, occasional use a few times a week, a
              well-built home-tier treadmill will likely serve you just as
              well for meaningfully less money.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do commercial-grade treadmills come with better warranties?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Generally yes, especially on motor and frame coverage. It's
              common to see <strong>lifetime or 10-plus-year</strong> frame
              warranties and multi-year motor coverage on true commercial
              machines, reflecting the manufacturer's confidence in the build
              quality, compared to shorter frame and motor coverage windows
              typical of home-tier treadmills.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do commercial treadmills require special electrical or floor
              setup?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Some do. Higher-powered AC motors on the largest commercial
              machines can require a dedicated circuit, and the heavier fixed
              frames benefit from a level, reinforced floor, particularly on
              upper stories. It's worth checking a specific model's
              electrical and flooring requirements before purchase rather
              than assuming standard household wiring and flooring will
              suffice.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Can a folding treadmill ever be considered commercial-grade?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Rarely in the strictest sense, since most true commercial-duty
              machines use fixed frames for maximum rigidity. Some folding
              treadmills borrow commercial-grade components like stronger
              motors and reinforced decks without carrying a full commercial
              duty-cycle rating, offering a middle ground between home-tier
              and true commercial performance for buyers who also need to
              save space.
            </p>
